retion around a rotating compact object, whether a neutron star or a black hole, is largely determined by the angle at which the wind escapes as a result of the object's rotation. However, the angle is itself affected by the density of the surrounding wind. To accurately model this feedback, researchers use a sophisticated equation that incorporates information about the rotation speed, the angle at which the wind escapes, the viscosity of the wind, and the density of the surrounding wind. Using a simple simulation of a wind stream, researchers demonstrated the importance of considering this feedback in models of subsonic wind accretion. They found that even a small change in density can lead to a significant change in the rate of wind accretion, especially in the inner regions. In fact, the feedback loop can cause a buildup of dense wind in the inner regions, a phenomenon that has not been observed in previous studies that ignored this feedback. Furthermore, this feedback can exacerbate the phenomenon of subsonic wind streams being interrupted by brief episodes of supersonic accretion. } 
